nd, skip closes with the limitation noted. Option 2: a scoped yin.vm-probe design round + architect review, then the full-fidelity fix.\\norchestrator-logs/1793385006-zcode-glm-5.3-flash-yang-clojure-track.md:529:Done: (1) The astra second opinion (1794320000000-...gpt-6-astra.stdout.log) returned: do NOT adopt position-only selection as written — it breaks the LANDED clj_apply callback leg (cross_lang_test.clj:100, a TS closure passed as a parameter has no projected-name position) and the §8.10.11 contract (\\\"pass, return, store, recurse, call back\\\"); the scissor framing was overstated (a wrong-seal imitation IS heap-distinguishable; only an exact copy of a valid ref is indistinguishable — and that is a working bearer reference by design); verdict: retain the two operations, rule for a generic non-fatal authentication effect reusing the engine's existing authentic-ref? (engine.cljc:331), with five rules (strict explicit resolution; runtime authentication in ordinary application; authentication ≠ authorization per the :2120 pin; bearer copies retain behavior; static specialization is optimization-not-semantics). (2) The owner asked what \\\"authentication effect\\\" means; seat grounded it in the codebase: the cell module is an effect language (pure constructors + engine interpreter, module.cljc:378); authentic? = a fourth constructor + one interpreter case returning (authentic-ref? state :cell/ref ...) non-fatally — the query voice of the predicate that today only speaks as check-ref!'s fatal gate. Owner: \\\"oh i see. monadic effect ... ok. i like this idea. lets go with your recommendation\\\" — RULING ACCEPTED. (3) Ruling-of-record written (collab/yang-clojure/1794330000000-architect-cell-ref-ruling-of-record.md): the seven rules incl. scope decisions (entry-key policy OUT — rule 7; the bag clarification: storage changes nothing, the authentication query at the call decides) + required §8.10 documentation + the acceptance battery. (4) r5 dispatched to glm-5