ment ends, so every loop decision re-consumes the rest of the statement — 64,024,002 consumptions for a 16,002-token 8,000-operator chain; a counter of closure steps and rule entries alone sees the chain's work linear, measured) — refused as status -7 `work <measured> <bound>` (the same status as the nesting guards, not -8: the message names the axis), `{:diagnostic :parser-budget :axis :work}`; no unit throws inside a full-context prediction (its consume would unwind past the reach computation the leak fix protects); checked again at every prediction's entry and after recognition; a work refusal resets the worker's parser DFA so a refused source refuses on retry; `kMaxWork` 600,000,000, calibrated (`bb calibrate:jolt-j5 --work`, build/j5b/work.edn) from the measured maximum over the whole parsing corpus — the gated 20,000-operator `1+` chain at 400,129,567 units is the floor — with the adversarial curves above it (`1*`x4,000 about 8.1e8, refuses mid-walk; `1+` past 24,491 operators refuses); the corpus, the at-bound nesting shapes, the 4 MiB flat inputs and the gated flat sizes parse unchanged, zero parity pins. Divergences from the row's plan recorded: the 10 s refusal walls were infeasible with the floor the existing gates set (refusing at 600,000,000 units costs the time those units cost — about 20 s for the `1+` family, minutes-becoming-tens-of-seconds for the `1*` walk), and the bound is not per-byte (per-byte cannot separate a 24 KB `1*` chain from a 24 KB legitimate source). The lexer's per-NEWLINE constant (two `std::regex` constructions per newline in the generated lexer base) is linear in work but unbounded in wall: disclosed in §7.6, not bounded. Linux evidence pending (the orchestrator closes this row); precondition for parsing remotely sourced Python (J6) | P2 |\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:350:| yang.clojure: the slice-9 kernel differential gate is honestly blocked at **107/165 credited (39 direct + 68 named integrations), 58 unexercised admitted arities**, 100 agreements, 2 recorded deviations (`elab-after-qed`, `check-after-qed`), 2 blocked fixtures, 2 declared upstream failures; the earlier 145/165-and-20 accounting predated the common-call reconciliation and under-counted (38 named-integration trace paths no longer exist) | hardening-3 H3-0 (the architect's finding: the deviation classifier required a bare closure where the reconciled compiler publishes the admitted §8.10 descriptor) | yang.clojure | H3-0 repaired the bookkeeping (versioned slice9_declarations.edn drives a representation-aware classifier with declared-call, message and script revalidation; the writer carries declared dispositions; a writer round-trip test replays the checked-in evidence) — repair, not closure; H3-1..H3-5 close the 58 per the approved hardening-3 plan | P2 |\\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:397:## 2026-10-29 19:5x +0700 — the session-id collision lesson logged; the cross-language demo and the 8.10 spec distinction recorded\\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:400:Done: The owner asked two questions that produced recorded findings: (1) why no terminals are visible (the delegates run as background processes writing to collab/ logs, not interactive terminals; the shiva lanes run in tmux on the remote server), and (2) whether the section 8.10 cross-language calling convention spec is unnecessary given that cross-language calls already work. The answer to (2): no — the demo proved the mechanism (the substrate routes calls correctly), but the spec defines the semantics (null sentinels, number kinds, opaque handles, references, exceptions, cleanup, ownership). The calls work at the engine level; the contract for what values mean at the boundary didn't exist until the spec defined it. The distinction: \\\"the pipe works\\\" vs \\\"what flows through the pipe and what happens when it leaks.\\\"\\norchestrator-logs/1793179048-yang-php-yang-typescript-glm-resume.md:402