`getEpsilonTarget` closure step and every consumed token (the unit that sees the `1+` quadratic: prediction's DFA walk does not accept until its statement ends, so every loop decision re-consumes the rest of the statement — 64,024,002 consumptions for a 16,002-token 8,000-operator chain; a counter of closure steps and rule entries alone sees the chain's work linear, measured) — refused as status -7 `work <measured> <bound>` (the same status as the nesting guards, not -8: the message names the axis), `{:diagnostic :parser-budget :axis :work}`; no unit throws inside a full-context prediction (its consume would unwind past the reach computation the leak fix protects); checked again at every prediction's entry and after recognition; a work refusal resets the worker's parser DFA so a refused source refuses on retry; `kMaxWork` 600,000,000, calibrated (`bb calibrate:jolt-j5 --work`, build/j5b/work.edn) from the measured maximum over the whole parsing corpus — the gated 20,000-operator `1+` chain at 400,129,567 units is the floor — with the adversarial curves above it (`1*`x4,000 about 8.1e8, refuses mid-walk; `1+` past 24,491 operators refuses); the corpus, the at-bound nesting shapes, the 4 MiB flat inputs and the gated flat sizes parse unchanged, zero parity pins. Divergences from the row's plan recorded: the 10 s refusal walls were infeasible with the floor the existing gates set (refusing at 600,000,000 units costs the time those units cost — about 20 s for the `1+` family, minutes-becoming-tens-of-seconds for the `1*` walk), and the bound is not per-byte (per-byte cannot separate a 24 KB `1*` chain from a 24 KB legitimate source). The lexer's per-NEWLINE constant (two `std::regex` constructions per newline in the generated lexer base) is linear in work but unbounded in wall: disclosed in §7.6, not bounded. Linux evidence pending (the orchestrator closes this row); precondition for parsing remotely sourced Python (J6) | P2 |\\\\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:350:| yang.cl