gn/yang.clojure* docs/design/yang.antlr.md | head -70\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'J2|portable source|portable sources|budget|call.site' docs/design/yin.repl* docs/design/yang.clojure* docs/design/yang.antlr.md | head -70\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"zsh:1: no matches found: docs/design/yang.clojure*\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1830,1910p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md; nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-DRAFT.md; rg -n 'occurrence|application|:app|contract' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | head -70\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1830,1910p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md; nl -ba collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-DRAFT.md; rg -n 'occurrence|application|:app|contract' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | head -70\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"### 8.1 The module manifest\\n\\nA manifest is content: one map, materialized in `dao.jing`, named by its\\naddress. It replaces the `{sym fn}` dictionary entry.\\n\\n```clojure\\n{:yin.module/name        'my.lib\\n :yin.module/schema      1                   ; this container's shape\\n :yin.module/contracts   {:yin.ast/code          \\\"v3\\\"   ; per format\\n                          :yin.semantic/code     \\\"v3\\\"\\n                          :yin.debruijn.code     \\\"b2\\\"\\n                          :yin.debruijn.register \\\"r2\\\"}\\n :yin.module/tree        :segment/...        ; the one canonical tree\\n :yin.module/derivations {:yin.semantic/code     :segment/...  ; record\\n                          :yin.debruijn.code     :segment/...  ; record\\n                          :yin.debruijn.register :segment/...} ; record\\n :yin.module/index       {H :segment/... R :segment/...}       ; optional\\n :yin.module/exports     #{'foo 'bar}\\n :yin.module/requires    {'other.lib :segment/...}             ; manifest\\n :yin.module/primitives  {'println :yin.k.pp/blake3-...}       ; profile\\n :yin.module/footprint   {:store-keys #{} :effects #{}}}       ; UCF 7.6.1\\n```\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n- **One tree.** `:yin.module/tree` is the root row id of the canonical\\n  AST the module was authored as. The `:yin.ast/code` image *is* this\\n  tree, so it has no derivation record.\\n- **One derivation record per lowered format.** Each value under\\n  `:yin.module/derivations` is the address of a content-addressed\\n  derivation record in the shape `yin.vm.ledger/derive-record` mints:\\n  `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree\\n  :yin.ledger/output identity :yin.ledger/function f\\n  :yin.ledger/profile {...}}`. The linker fetches the record (through a\\n  `:yin.ledger/record` format record: `segment-matches?` identity, a\\n  validator over these keys, no parts, no obligations) and checks\\n  `:yin.ledger/input` equals `:yin.module/tree` and `:yin.ledger/output`\\n  is the identity it then fetches. A record leading from a different\\n  tree, or to a different identity, is `:derivation-mismatch`. Four\\n  individually valid images from four different trees therefore cannot\\n  share a manifest, and a swapped image fails before load.\\n- **Three contracts, kept apart.** A manifest carries a schema version\\n  and a per-format contract map, and neither is the other:\\n  - `:yin.module/schema` versions the manifest container itself: which\\n    keys exist and what they mean. It is checked by the\\n    `:yin.module/manifest` format record's validator, which implements\\n    exactly one schema version; a manifest of another version is\\n    `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:schema`. It says nothing about any\\n    bytecode.\\n  - `:yin.module/contracts` maps each format the manifest lowered to\\n    the execution contract that format's image targets: the UCF stamp\\n    `\\\"v3\\\"` for the semantic vector and the tree, the stack lowering\\n    contract `\\\"b2\\\"` for the stack image, the register contract `\\\"r2\\\"`\\n    for the register image. These are the Rule R revisions; a manifest\\n    naming \\\"v2\\\", \\\"b1\\\", or \\\"r1\\\" is `:contract-mismatch`, with no\\n    migration. A format present under `:yin.module/derivations`\\n    must be present here; one that is not is `:descriptor-defect` with\\n    rule `:contract-missing`.\\n  - A format record's `:contract` (section 4.1) is the one execution\\n    contract that record implements, and a request's\\n    `:yin.link/contract` is the one the requester runs.\\n  **Contract before content.** Immediately after the manifest passes\\n  steps 2 to 4, `(get :yin.module/contracts format)` for the requested\\n  format is checked against the format record's `:contract`\\n  (`:contract-mismatch`), before the name check and before any\\n  derivation or image is fetched (section 4.2, step 0). A four-way\\n  manifest linking through the stack kernel therefore compares `\\\"b2\\\"`\\n  with `\\\"b2\\\"`, and the same manifest linking through the semantic\\n  kernel compares `\\\"v3\\\"` with `\\\"v3\\\"`; the tree's `\\\"v3\\\"` and the register\\n  image's `\\\"r2\\\"` play no part in either check. Revision r3 carried one\\n  `:yin.module/contract \\\"v2\\\"` and would have refused every stack and\\n  register link of a valid manifest; that was a defect.\\n- **Verifying and trusting are two policies, and verifying never\\n  trusts.** The linker's `:derivation` policy is one of `:verifying` or\\n  `:trusted`, chosen by the composition; there is no policy under which\\n  a verifying link accepts a claim it did not recompute.\\n  - Under `:verifying`, for every image it delivers the linker fetches\\n    the manifest's tree through the `:yin.ast/code` record (steps 2 to\\n    4, so the tree itself is verified, and no further: the tree's own\\n    obligations are the walker's business, scanned only when the tree is\\n    the requested image), re-lowers it under the exact\\n     1\\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-05 04:30:00 GMT\\n     2\\tStatus: ADDENDUM A to the signed attribution-as-query ruling (1794600000000-...ruling-of-record.md) — DRAFT, pending gpt-6-astra review, then owner signature\\n     3\\t# ADDENDUM A — call-site annotation generalizes: every yang.* compilation pipeline annotates the call site\\n     4\\t\\n     5\\t## The owner's directive (recorded verbatim in intent)\\n     6\\t\\n     7\\t\\\"the other compilers must also add more information to the compilation pipeline by annotating the callsite.\\\" The site provenance defined by this ruling — caller-site identity at application expressions, post-macroexpansion — is hereby the **declared standard for every yang.* frontend**, not an oracle-local mechanism. The oracle's instantiation (in flight) is the first consumer; the other frontends' adoption is scheduled direction, not a hypothetical.\\n     8\\t\\n     9\\t## What generalizes, and to where\\n    10\\t\\n    11\\t1. **The semantics generalize unchanged**: caller-site identity, assigned at application expressions post-macroexpansion, carried on the callee-entry record; consumers resolve sites against declared/derived tables. The oracle's R2 mechanism is instantiation #1 of exactly this contract.\\n    12\\t2. **The representation home generalizes to UAST**: for frontends to emit site ids natively, the UAST application-node representation gains the annotation (a §8.1 contract revision — the linker's derivation/contract machinery already versions formats). This is the architectural difference between instantiation #1 (harness-level, oracle-owned) and the generalization (representation-level, VM-owned) — and it needs its own design round before any frontend lands it.\\n    13\\t3. **The second consumer is now SCHEDULED, which unblocks R1's deferred half**: the durable correspondence record (R1's descoped address-linked adaptation manifest) lands alongside the generalization — its trigger condition (\\\"a second consumer exists\\\") is satisfied by this directive by owner declaration.\\n    14\\t\\n    15\\t## Sequencing (proposed)\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\t1. The oracle round (in flight) lands instantiation #1 — unchanged by this addendum.\\n    18\\t2. The generalization DESIGN round: the UAST application-node annotation (representation, contracts revision, per-frontend lowering changes, budget reviews per track) — architect-reviewed before any frontend dispatches.\\n    19\\t3. Per-frontend adoption rounds (typescript, php, python; jolt inherits through the portable sources) — each with its own gate; the cell-ref sweeps and all current lanes are unaffected and proceed unchanged.\\n    20\\t4. R1's durable correspondence record lands with (2)/(3) — additive to the oracle's site tables, per R1's condition (b) (the versioned evidence format).\\n    21\\t\\n    22\\t## Boundaries\\n    23\\t\\n    24\\t- No existing rule changes: the signed ruling's R1-R7 stand as written; this addendum schedules the generalization and unblocks R1's deferred half.\\n    25\\t- The in-flight sweeps (cell-ref, ts/php/python), the jolt J2 re-run, and the oracle round are unaffected.\\n    26\\t- Budget impact per frontend is a design-round deliverable, not assumed here.\\n    27\\t\\n    28\\t## What astra is asked to review\\n    29\\t\\n    30\\t1. Is the UAST application-node representation the right home for the annotation (vs per-frontend lowering-level tags)? What does the §8.1 contracts revision require?\\n    31\\t2. Does scheduling R1's durable record against the generalization hold, or should it precede/follow differently?\\n    32\\t3. Per-frontend sequencing and budget-review obligations — anything the design round must measure first?\\n    33\\t4. Any interaction with the in-flight seats' work (the sweeps, the J2 re-run, the jolt host) that this addendum must call out?\\n    34\\t5. Verdict: endorse the addendum for owner signature, or amend.\\n18:[`datom.md`](./datom.md) (the datom contract: stream-local `e`/`t`/`m`,\\n23:(UCF: the canonical instruction vector §7.3.2, contract stamp §7.3.3,\\n43:historical; its stance and contract history survive in the renamed\\n59:|                | the Map AST semantic root      | a linearized bytecode of flat       | address — a row's  | address                  | name, no occurrence, no        |\\n73:|                |                                | and the occurrence relation (§6.1)  |                    |                          |                                |\\n90:and the occurrence relation (`§6.1`) — and wherever this document says\\n106:| provenance and custody | derive, expand, and publish events, UCF's occurrence ledgers and leases: facts a medium possesses; a hash possesses nothing        |\\n125:- **Nothing about time, naming, occurrence, or provenance enters code\\n140:- **Content identity is not occurrence identity.** An address says *what*\\n142:  expansion attempt produced it is occurrence information, and it is kept\\n163: :body {:type :application,\\n169:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\\n182:[B :application C [D E] true]\\n246:| `key`   | a store key: any value of the    | the store contract is a map lookup with no key restriction (`ast_walker.cljc:401`, `:402-404`); the linearizer admits any |\\n288:| :application            | 4          | [:application operator operands      | operator operands tail?  | node, nodes, bool| :371-376           | :424-429        |\\n330:**`tail?` is a slot of `:application` only.** The one reader of the tail\\n332:(`linearize.cljc:107-108`), which reads it on `:application`; the walker\\n339:mark on an `:if`'s branches is carried by the branch `:application` nodes\\n361:| `:application` tail?                               | `false`                             | codec emits only `true` (`v2.cljc:412`); the linearizer reads `(boolean …)` |\\n364:| `:application` / `:dao.stream.apply/call` operands | `[]`                                | walker `ast_walker.cljc:383`                                                |\\n367:### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\\n384:  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\\n405:- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\\n411:  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\\n426:An **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\\n430:is keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\\n436:removes. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\\n453:| harvest catalogue      | `[[:source medium batch] h j path]`: occurrence `[j path]` belongs to the `h`-th **original definition** in the        | the frontend or the  |\\n512:  (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`), while an application interprets an\\n519:  effect descriptor under the applicable execution contract**; a result\\n520:  the contract would interpret is stored by the arm and executed by the\\n568:| lowering as an address-to-address function    | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and each application of it is one derivation record (§5.2)                        |\\n596:contract precisely and its remaining residuals: scalar (symbol) metadata\\n636:### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\\n645:`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\\n659:\\\"This call site\\\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\\n661:joins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\\n663:or a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\\n665:occurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\\n683:occurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\\n696:cosmetic; the no-fallthrough contract (`:variable`'s lexical-only\\n715:`:application`'s operator/operands slots), not generic over §2.3's full\\n725:at one occurrence and bound at another — and this is not a permanent\\n728:free at one occurrence and bound at another (e.g. `((fn [x] x) x)`, whose\\n733:which would silently drop the genuinely free occurrence from a computed\\n741:*specific occurrence* is exactly a places-not-contents question, and\\n742:§6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\\n744:each occurrence's own path to its ancestors, not the row's abstracted\\n746:`occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\\n748:occurrence relation of §2.5's shape as `yin.vm/occurrences` emits it, a\\n754:richer, occurrence-joined query, exactly as this section claims.\\n756:**Root-scoped, and portable data.** A production occurrence relation\\n757:holds every reachable tree's occurrences together, and structural paths\\n761:misclassify an occurrence in one tree against a binder in another;\\n764:`yin.vm/occurrence-rules`, is one non-recursive rule over explicitly\\n778:A name is bound at `[root path]` iff a `:lambda` occurrence **of the same\\n790:`occurrence-walk-covers-the-whole-grammar` resolves binding through `:if`\\n792:emits the occurrence tuples is `yin.vm/occurrences`.\\n802:slot of a two-operand application whose first operand is a literal\\n803:symbol other than `yin/def`; every other occurrence, a `:lambda` binding\\n826:strictly retain named variables. Therefore, every contract name in this\\n836:tree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\\n850:                       :yin.code/contract \\\"v3\\\"  ; UCF S7.3.3 contract\\n861:UCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\\n864:two deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\\n873:| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\\n882:| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\\n886:contract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\\n888:execution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\\n907:   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'J2|portable sources|call.site' docs/design/yin.repl.md docs/design/yang.antlr.md docs/design/yin.vm.jolt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_11\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'J2|portable sources|call.site' docs/design/yin.repl.md docs/design/yang.antlr.md docs/design/yin.vm.jolt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"rg: docs/design/yin.repl.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\nrg: docs/design/yin.vm.jolt.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:3529:UAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:3699:PHP call sites include it when their declared target has reference\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:3746:may remain as source call-site helpers, but must not reject an admitted\\n\",\"exit_code\":2,\"status\":\"failed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '115,148p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '367,445p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '636,669p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; rg --files docs/design | rg 'jolt|repl|clojure'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_12\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '115,148p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '367,445p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '636,669p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; rg --files docs/design | rg 'jolt|repl|clojure'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"(traces, stream facts, stigmergy, schema, data) is datoms; this design only\\npulled code out of them and made it properly immutable.\\n\\nSaid plainly: the datom layer is not a description of the code; it is the\\nonly place the code's name, past, and ownership exist. Content says what\\nthe program is; datoms say which program we mean, where it came from, and\\nwho may run it.\\n\\nWhat never crosses:\\n\\n- **Nothing about time, naming, occurrence, or provenance enters code\\n  content.** `t`, `m`, entity ids, source positions, the macro flag, the\\n  expansion that produced a tree, the AST a segment came from, and the\\n  address itself are all outside the hashed value. UCF §7.3.2 already\\n  excludes these from the instruction vector; §2.5 applies the same\\n  exclusion to the AST. The consequence is stated, not hidden: code\\n  content does not know its predecessor, so the chain between values is\\n  held by ledger records, never by the values themselves (§8.2).\\n- **No code content enters a ref.** A ref carries an address and nothing\\n  else about the code; two ledgers that agree on `dao.jing` addresses\\n  interoperate without exchanging trees.\\n- **No datom-projection tempid is ever named by a ref, a ledger row, a\\n  closure, or a continuation.** The datom projection's `e` values are\\n  local; only addresses cross layers, and this is what retires the\\n  `:eid` sharing of `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:394-411` (§4.4).\\n- **Content identity is not occurrence identity.** An address says *what*\\n  a value is. Which source position, which batch, which call site, which\\n  expansion attempt produced it is occurrence information, and it is kept\\n  in side tables and events keyed by an origin (source batch or producing\\n  event), root address, and structural path (§2.5, §4.4, §8.4), never by\\n  content address alone.\\n- **The hot loop reads content only.** An evaluator builds its image at\\n  load — the walker reconstructs the map AST from rows, the semantic VM\\n  decodes the vector — and never consults a ref or a projection while\\n### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\\n\\nExclusion is a rule of the projection boundary, the same rule §2.4 states\\nfor saturation: these facts are stripped map→rows and never re-derived\\nrows→map. The map AST a frontend emits carries them; no row does:\\n\\n- **Source positions** and any frontend metadata (`:yang/*` keys, Clojure\\n  reader metadata on symbols). Tracked instead in secondary side tables.\\n  **Parameter and variable names are not an exclusion**: the boundary\\n  projection retains them in the canonical tuples (`:lambda params`,\\n  `:variable name`), since this design preserves named variables across\\n  the boundary.\\n- **`:macro?`** and `:phase-policy`. `yang.clojure` sets them on defmacro\\n  lambdas (`clojure.cljc:436-438`) and the codec persists `:yin/macro?`\\n  (`v2.cljc:419-420`, schema `v2.cljc:312`). No evaluator reads them\\n  (`ast_walker.cljc:20-23`). The expander needs the fact that a particular\\n  definition is a macro; that fact travels beside the tree as an\\n  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\\n- **`:eid`** (§4.4).\\n- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\\n  the tree names the root. No root fact exists in a row — the root row's\\n  id is the root fact (§4.1).\\n- **`t`, `m`, provenance.** Every provenance fact the codec or linearizer\\n  writes today (`:yin.code/source`, `:yin.code/derived-from`) becomes a\\n  ledger record (§8) or a side-table entry.\\n\\n**Occurrences.** Content identity names *what*; three further things\\nname *where*, and each is a distinct coordinate:\\n\\n- A **structural path** names a place inside one tree: a vector of slot\\n  indices from the root, with an index into a `nodes` slot written as a\\n  pair `[slot i]`. `[]` is the root; `[2 [3 0]]` is the first operand of\\n  the root's second slot. Slot indices are the row's positions, which the\\n  §2.3 dictionary fixes against the map's key order, so a path reads\\n  identically on both sides of the round trip. A path is a pure function\\n  of the tree, stable across media and hosts. `[root-address path]`\\n  separates two identical subtrees at two places in one tree, and nothing\\n  more.\\n- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\\n  j]`, the program medium's identity, the batch coordinate on it (the\\n  `:yin/source-batch` of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1), and the **member index**\\n  `j` of the tree within the batch (§8.5's ordered batch carries several\\n  trees). Two identical trees parsed from two files, admitted in two\\n  batches, or sitting at two indices of one batch have one root address\\n  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\\n  source, and neither does a batch without its member index.\\n  Here `medium` is the program medium's `:dao.stream/identity` and `batch`\\n  is the admitting composition's fresh plain-data token, carried as\\n  `:yin/source-medium` and `:yin/batch-token` in the batch envelope. A retry\\n  of an unaccepted staged append keeps that token; a distinct admission\\n  mints another. Neither coordinate is inferred from cursor internals or\\n  content.\\n- A **producing event** names one generated tree: the `:expand` event\\n  (§8.4) whose output it is. Two expansion attempts with identical output\\n  have one output address and two producing events. An event has two\\n  identities, used in two places: a **log-local entity id**, which only a\\n  declared reference attribute on the same log may name, and the\\n  **address of its expansion record** (§8.2), which is portable.\\n\\nAn **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\\n`origin` is `[:source medium batch j]` for admitted syntax and\\n`[:expansion record-address]` for generated syntax. Every side table and\\nevery provenance link that must distinguish places, sources, or attempts\\nis keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\\nindex travels in the origin uniformly: declarations, source positions,\\ninstruction provenance, and initial expansion events all carry the same\\n`[:source medium batch j]`. Joining a position table to a batch table on\\nroot address is not a substitute: it yields every batch-position\\ncombination, which is exactly the ambiguity the origin coordinate\\nremoves. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\\nkey, because a key travels and the number does not (§8.4).\\n\\n+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\\n| Side table             | Row                                                                                                                    | Keeper               |\\n+========================+========================================================================================================================+======================+\\n| source positions       | `[[:source medium batch j] root-address path file line col]`                                                           | the frontend that    |\\n|                        |                                                                                                                        | parsed the file      |\\n+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\\n| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\\n### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\\n\\nToday a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned\\nentity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda\\n(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it\\n(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each\\ncall-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\\n\\nContent addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one\\n`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\\naddress by construction and one row in the stored set, and a query \\\"which\\nsites reference this lambda's content\\\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\\nBecause this design retains named variables in the canonical tuple (§2, §5.1),\\nthis structural sharing requires exact identity, not alpha-equivalence:\\ntwo functions that are identical up to variable renaming project to\\n*different* rows, since the parameter and variable names are part of the\\nhashed content. Only subtrees identical in structure *and* in every retained\\nname collapse to one row. The row set holds each such distinct subtree once,\\nand parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\\n**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\\n`:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the codec.\\n\\nWhat `:eid` also did, and content addresses do not, is name a *place*.\\n\\\"This call site\\\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\\nnot the site's content address; a query that needs distinct call sites\\njoins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\\nlink that must survive a second identical site, a second identical file,\\nor a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\\naddresses serve sharing and cross-media correspondence of content;\\noccurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\\n\\n### 4.5 Free variables are queried, not tagged\\n\\nAn earlier revision of this design split variable references into two\\ndocs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md\\ndocs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md\\ndocs/design/yin-repl-design.md\\ndocs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I found a contract conflict for Astra to resolve: the code-as-tuples design already defines call-site identity as an occurrence key and keeps provenance outside hashed AST content. The addendum needs to distinguish annotating an application occurrence from adding a canonical application-node field.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n '\\\"'^##|''^###|caller|site|UAST|schema|version'\\\"' collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n '\\\"'^##|''^###|caller|site|UAST|schema|version'\\\"' collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"5:# Task: implement the SIGNED attribution-as-query ruling — caller-side site tagging, derived site tables, the seven-case battery, the honest migration\\n9:## Context\\n11:Worktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-aaq, branch attribution-as-query, base master 3eebe844e (the ruling landed; D2, cell/authentic?, H3-2, H3-3 all in). The governing reviews (astra/qwen/kimi) are linked from the ruling's header — kimi's v3 review found the mechanism flaw your implementation must respect: **site ids are CALLER-SIDE, at application expressions in the compiled AST, post-macroexpansion; the callee-entry record carries the CALLER's site id.** Callee-side tagging (v3's flaw) yields identical records for both origins and is forbidden.\\n13:## The implementation, in the ruling's stated order\\n15:1. **FIRST DELIVERABLE — the written inventory confirmation**: what the instrumentation change touches (kernel-trace-form's position, the walker's application-node stamping site, the callee-record extension), the capacity measurement (stream/make + the instruction bound vs the added trace field — R6), and the offset-table coverage list (every context-API arity kernel-context-extra-arguments served, latte_oracle_test.clj:1786). Write it BEFORE code; commit it as the round's first commit.\\n16:2. **Caller-side site-id stamping**: application expressions in the compiled AST (post-macroexpansion — the corpus runs under is/testing/LaTTe macros; source-level tagging misses expanded sites) get compile-time-unique site ids; the callee-entry record becomes `[fname arity caller-site-id]`. The walker changes are AUTHORIZED by the ruling (R2) — scoped to application-node stamping + the record extension; nothing else in yin.vm moves.\\n17:3. **Mechanically derived site tables**: the compiler derives each site's argument shape (ambient literal `empty-env` vs anything else) during instrumentation — the tables are DERIVED, not hand-declared (R7's trust model), recorded in a stable versioned evidence format (R1's condition b), with the canonical-source-hash per fixture (R2b case 3's referent).\\n23:## Verification (test-boundary ruling: focused local, suite-scale remote)\\n29:## Boundaries\\n31:- yin.vm: ONLY the application-node stamping + the callee-record extension (R2's authorization). No other VM changes. yang.clojure: untouched unless the stamping requires it (justify). The harness (latte_oracle_test.clj, context.edn, slice9_gate.md's versioned attribution section) is the main surface. The pin migrations are authorized by R5 — list every revised pin in the evidence.\\n34:## Report\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'J2|portable|budget|contract|rebuild' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md | head -65; sed -n '3510,3545p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'application|occurrence' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast.cljc src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc | head -30\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'J2|portable|budget|contract|rebuild' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md | head -65; sed -n '3510,3545p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'application|occurrence' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast.cljc src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc | head -30\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"5:VM, not a source-to-jolt compiler and not a new dao.stream contract. The\\n7:contracts and conformance oracle stay shared. jolt branches are added only where\\n19:walks (`f6e29391`); J2 test lane, CBOR host seam and libcrypto random bytes\\n21:not merged: the UDP probe stays on `jolt-j0b` (`6fa97400`). J2 is closed\\n22:(2026-10-24, master `f4d6a284`): J2 rerun 3 on shiva at `078e1e43` ran all 150\\n36:host-boundary seat's independent focused run (16/217/0). After J2 (2026-10-24):\\n40:deftype now throws, as on the JVM; 156 included / 53 excluded); J2b is closed\\n44:and the JavaScript/Dart lexer-helper manifest patches. J2b closing is necessary\\n72:namespace requires `dao.stream.transit`; the portable domain moves to a\\n121:  (\\\"implementations do not add contracts\\\").\\n124:  the landed datagram contract.\\n128:  contract and the K0/W0 fixtures (§10.4).\\n169:- No performance budget. Benchmarks are reported from J0 on; thresholds are\\n273:  surface and contract tests.\\n294:guard before `:clj`. The portable `dao.stream.ws` and `dao.stream.datagram` do\\n310:JS/Dart roots. `:replace-deps` holds the portable source dependencies this lane\\n319:and test aliases, bootstrap/native tasks and J2 lane (`0d07e55d`, `a0a49b8e`).\\n363:verified starting set, not a claim that the 91-namespace closure is portable.\\n461:| UDP                            | Narrow nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM adapter under the landed datagram contract (§8).                                          | J0b, J7     |\\n463:| WebSocket                      | Native RFC 6455 host adapter supplying the full host contract, CBOR subprotocol only (§9).                              | J6          |\\n488:  apply the one-bit truth table, rebuild with exact powers; a result with bit\\n524:overlay `boring.cljc:50`) is a wire-writer node-contract mismatch: the `:clj`\\n561:landed in J2; this does not close J4's file or authority integration gates.\\n566:  same input array**, which is returned. Keep the comparator's sign contract and\\n652:portable C implementation; SIMD is a separately tested optimization.\\n655:(`test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\\n720:gate. No numeric JVM-ratio budget exists before J8 evidence.\\n836:`yang/python/antlr/repl.cljc:26-32` is portable lowering. Removing one catalog\\n956:nesting/resource budget for that exact thread and build across bracket,\\n1019:  destroyed on the caller's stack. The §7.5 contract is unchanged: jolt makes\\n1073:sourced Python (J6), slice J5c adds a deterministic work budget (prediction\\n1081:A refusal is operational: `{:diagnostic :parser-budget :axis :input|:nesting\\n1091:Platforms. J5b's budget, crash, calibration and time legs ran on macOS arm64\\n1095:On Linux x86_64 (shiva, 2026-10-29) the budget and below legs ran green at the\\n1100:prediction reach set, which leaks once per parser-budget refusal because the\\n1135:`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`; the owner split these into J2b on 2026-10-22\\n1195:### 8.1 The landed datagram contract (owner Q7)\\n1197:Owner: \\\"keep landed contract\\\". V: `src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc:25-33`\\n1267:datagram-contract and DHT/linker composition are the main effort:\\n1283:### 9.2 The host contract (W1)\\n1288:contract, not only the per-connection map:\\n1346:- the full host contract of §9.2, with per-connection ownership and no callback\\n1398:  reason \\\"Transit codec, owner-ruled out on jolt\\\" in the J2 applicability\\n1510:landings and J0c's probe do not discharge J2b or J5 acceptance.\\n1536:| probe-only; landed                          | and helpers,      | restricted lowering corpus, two-worker/cache isolation, collect-safe calls, thread/stack budget,    |\\n1554:| J2: pure test lane and VM conformance       | `test:jolt`       | Every applicable pure test loads and passes; semantic and register boundary traces match. No silent |\\n1565:| J2b: deferred comparisons                   | Oracle/lowering   | Node/Dart oracle comparison and restricted lowering comparison (§7.8); both close before J5. Owner  |\\n1566:| closed (zero pins, JVM canonical)           |                   | split from J2, 2026-10-22 (§13.5); JVM canonical, divergent hosts patched (owner 2026-10-24).       |\\n1574:|                                             | catalog, shell    | selectable since J5 (the native C++ parser, §7.6 budget).                                           |\\n1582:| J4 closed 2026-10-28;                       |                   | never overwritten; no J4-owned entry remains in jolt-applicability.edn or the J2 lane; cross-host   |\\n1590:|                                             | ness-probe        | Amended (row 59): enforced input/nesting budget, stage-owned worker lifecycle and the               |\\n1592:|                                             |                   | 16 MiB parse thread (§7.6). J5b budget/crash legs (bb probe:jolt-j5, bb calibrate:jolt-j5) on       |\\n1594:|                                             |                   | the owner's \\\"linux only\\\" waiver. J5c (deterministic work budget) ledgered; precondition for         |\\n1603:|                                             | contract, CBOR    |                                                                                                     |\\n1608:|                                             | datagram contract |                                                                                                     |\\n1637:J0a, J0b and J0c are independently commissionable and reportable; their\\n1693:their portable behavioural equivalents and cross-process peer legs are required.\\n1697:lane table remains open. `test:jolt-core` includes `^:slow`; J2 gate (b) remains\\n1699:pending (`a0a49b8e`; `docs/agents/known-gaps.md`, J2 classification rows).\\n1711:budget. At J8: startup cold/warm/devboot/AOT, submission latency, large-integer\\n1782:|            |                                       | never silently. No numeric JVM-ratio budgets until J8 evidence.                                                  |\\n1786:| Q7         | \\\"keep landed contract\\\"                | No UDP `full`. Would-block (EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK, macOS ENOBUFS) is transport-error plus the send-failed event; no |\\n1868:  resolved jolt closure. J2: codec-less compositions fail at composition on\\n1880:  `append!`; the contract forbids a waiting operation and the transport's\\n1956:closure. Evidence: `a0a49b8e` (J2 lane and its owner-ruling comments),\\n\\nC preprocessing, Rust macro expansion, SQL dialect resolution, TypeScript\\nchecking, and Ruby dynamic dispatch are plugin-owned stages. Each has\\nexplicit inputs, outputs, and dependencies. None requires Yang core to\\nrecognize its language by name.\\n\\n### 8.10 Shared function calling convention\\n\\n**Status: proposed, 2026-10-29; not implemented.** This section specifies\\none invocation convention for every conforming ANTLR-based Yang frontend.\\nExamples are non-exhaustive. Adding a language must not add a case to Yang\\ncore, a language-pair bridge, or an evaluator transition. The words\\n**must**, **must not**, and **may** state requirements on a future\\nconforming implementation, not claims about current code.\\n\\n#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\\n\\nA Yin function is a Yin function, independent of its source syntax. The\\nVM applies a closure to operands. Source calling semantics are ordinary\\nUAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\\n\\nEvery supported source-level callable, after its source-required\\nresolution, exposes `:yang.call/entry`, an ordinary Yin closure taking\\nexactly one invocation record. Invoking it is an ordinary\\n`:application`. No AST tag, lambda slot, instruction, host primitive,\\neffect kind, or source-language test is added to the VM.\\n\\nThis is an internal calling convention, not a transport, another system\\nABI, or a host callback interface. Calls between source languages inside\\none task do not themselves cross a host boundary. Portable I/O and\\ncross-task interaction continue through `dao.stream`.\\n\\n**Transparent calling** means that an admitted callable can be passed,\\nstored, returned, and invoked by any conforming frontend without a\\nhandwritten language-pair wrapper. It does not mean every foreign value\\nsatisfies every source operation. Invocation, name visibility, and value\\nrg: src/cljc/yin/vm/ast.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:4:   `:application`, `:if`, `:vm/current-continuation`); nothing here adds a\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:17:     (yin/def k v)     -> the definition application\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:18:     (f a ...)         -> :application\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:41:  {:type :application, :operator op, :operands (vec args)})\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Astra has confirmed that the implementation already has occurrence side tables and instruction provenance mappings. The remaining checks are rollout coverage—including production Clojure—and the compatibility and budget evidence required before adoption.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '845,910p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '1900,1940p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '845,910p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '1900,1940p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"{:yin.ledger/op       :derive\\n :yin.ledger/input    <tree-address>\\n :yin.ledger/output   <segment-address>\\n :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\\n :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"          ; the lowering profile, §5.2.1\\n                       :yin.code/contract \\\"v3\\\"  ; UCF S7.3.3 contract\\n                       :yin.k/version     0}}\\n```\\n\\nIts address is published in the ledger (§8.3). The chain\\n`record → input, record → output` is what \\\"the hash chain is the ledger\\\"\\nmeans under this design: the chain lives in ledger records and refs, not\\nin the code content, which rightly excludes it.\\n\\n#### 5.2.1 The lowering profile\\n\\nUCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\\nthe transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.\\nIts declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\\ntwo deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\\ndifferent, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable\\nonly against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:\\n\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Pinned by the profile                 | Reference value for `\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"`                                                                                                 |\\n+=======================================+================================================================================================================================+\\n| input grammar                         | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document                                                                             |\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| emission rules                        | the §5.3 flattening table of [the deleted v3 design](./yin.vm.semantic.md#history), in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right)    |\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| label and body order                  | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were  |\\n|                                       | encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`)                                                  |\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| terminators                           | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`)                                          |\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nThe profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution\\ncontract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\\nprofile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the\\nexecution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\\nchoice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured\\nmap above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection\\n(§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.\\n\\n#### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps\\n\\nA consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two\\ndifferent things and reports them separately:\\n\\n1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\\n   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the value. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and\\n   says nothing about the derivation.\\n2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's\\n   `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it lowers the reconstructed map under that profile and verifies the resulting instruction-vector address against `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality\\n   verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-\\n   mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A\\n   consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports\\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` naming both profiles, the same outcome UCF\\n   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\\n   output vector if the vector's own execution contract is one it\\n   implements, because content integrity (step 1) is independent of who\\n   lowered it.\\n  `:yin.module/contract \\\"v2\\\"` and would have refused every stack and\\n  register link of a valid manifest; that was a defect.\\n- **Verifying and trusting are two policies, and verifying never\\n  trusts.** The linker's `:derivation` policy is one of `:verifying` or\\n  `:trusted`, chosen by the composition; there is no policy under which\\n  a verifying link accepts a claim it did not recompute.\\n  - Under `:verifying`, for every image it delivers the linker fetches\\n    the manifest's tree through the `:yin.ast/code` record (steps 2 to\\n    4, so the tree itself is verified, and no further: the tree's own\\n    obligations are the walker's business, scanned only when the tree is\\n    the requested image), re-lowers it under the exact\\n    per-format profile the derivation record names (`\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\"`\\n    for `:yin.semantic/code`, the stack lowering for\\n    `:yin.debruijn.code`, the register lowering for\\n    `:yin.debruijn.register`, each pinned by its own\\n    `:yin.ledger/profile` map), and compares the recomputed identity to\\n    `:yin.ledger/output` (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 5.2.2, step\\n    2). Inequality is `:derivation-mismatch`. If the linker does not\\n    implement that exact profile, or the tree is `:absent`, or the tree\\n    fails its own verification, the link is refused\\n    `:unverified-derivation` naming the profile and what was missing.\\n    It is never downgraded to trust.\\n  - Under `:trusted`, the linker checks only the record's input and\\n    output addresses (the rule above) and reports `:trust :composition`\\n    in the outcome, exactly as B6's trusted fallback did and never\\n    silently. A composition that chooses `:trusted` has chosen to\\n    accept the publisher's lowering, and the outcome says so.\\n    `:trusted` skips **re-lowering**, never retrievability: the\\n    composition that serves under `:trusted` holds the manifest's\\n    `:yin.module/tree` address in its `source-index` and is responsible\\n    for the tree being retrievable from it (the publisher's store, or a\\n    store it can reach); no fetch is performed by the policy, and a tree\\n    whose read fails later is the medium's `:absent`, not a policy\\n    violation (`yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md` §9.2).\\n  The B6 fallback pair maps onto these: `verifying-fallback` is the\\n  `:verifying` policy applied to an H image selected in place of an\\n  unsupported R; `trusted-fallback` is `:trusted` applied the same way.\\n  The `:yin.ast/code` image needs no derivation under either policy: it\\n  is the tree, verified by content.\\n- **The name is verified.** Immediately after the manifest passes steps\\n  2 to 4, the linker compares `:yin.module/name` with the\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1205,1265p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; sed -n '180,222p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc; rg -n 'budget|baseline|instruction|trace capacity' docs/design/yang.typescript.md docs/design/yang.python.md docs/design/yang.php.md | head -50\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1205,1265p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; sed -n '180,222p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc; rg -n 'budget|baseline|instruction|trace capacity' docs/design/yang.typescript.md docs/design/yang.python.md docs/design/yang.php.md | head -50\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"                      :syms (mapcat\\n                              (fn [i sym]\\n                                (for [[k v] (sort-by (comp pr-str key)\\n                                                     (apply dissoc (or (meta sym) {})\\n                                                            source-position-keys))]\\n                                  [[field i k] v]))\\n                              (range) value)\\n                      nil)))\\n                slots)]\\n    (concat own names)))\\n\\n\\n(defn ast-side-tables\\n  \\\"Project occurrence-owned frontend facts from a map AST without putting\\n   them in canonical rows. Returns the §2.5 relations\\n   `{:source-positions [[origin root path file line col] ...]\\n     :frontend-metadata [[origin root path key value] ...]}`.\\n\\n   `bc` must be the projection of `ast`; its root makes both relations join\\n   the canonical row and occurrence relations. Structural paths use the same\\n   row positions as `occurrences` and lowering.\\\"\\n  [ast bc origin]\\n  (when-not (occurrence-origin? origin)\\n    (throw (ex-info \\\"Side tables require a ruled occurrence origin\\\"\\n                    {:rule :origin, :origin origin})))\\n  (let [root (:root bc)\\n        positions (atom [])\\n        metadata (atom [])]\\n    (letfn [(visit\\n              [node path]\\n              (let [tag (:type node)\\n                    slots (or (get semantic-bytecode-grammar tag)\\n                              (throw (ex-info \\\"Unknown AST node type\\\"\\n                                              {:type tag, :node node})))\\n                    position (reader-position node)]\\n                (when position\\n                  (swap! positions conj\\n                         [origin root path (:file position) (:line position)\\n                          (:column position)]))\\n                (doseq [[k v] (frontend-meta-entries tag node)]\\n                  (when-not (and (plain-data? k) (plain-data? v))\\n                    (throw (ex-info \\\"Frontend metadata is not plain data\\\"\\n                                    {:rule :frontend-metadata, :path path,\\n                                     :key k, :value v})))\\n                  (swap! metadata conj [origin root path k v]))\\n                (doseq [[j [field kind]] (map-indexed vector slots)\\n                        :let [pos (+ j 2)\\n                              value (get node field)]]\\n                  (case kind\\n                    :node (visit value (conj path pos))\\n                    :nodes (doseq [[i child] (map-indexed vector value)]\\n                             (visit child (conj path [pos i])))\\n                    nil))))]\\n      (visit ast [])\\n      {:source-positions @positions, :frontend-metadata @metadata})))\\n\\n\\n(defn- semantic-bytecode-slot-kind-ok?\\n  \\\"§2.2/§7.4 `:slot-kind`: whether `v` conforms to slot `kind`.\\\"\\n  [kind v]\\n  (case kind\\n  [rows]\\n  {:tag (fn [id] (nth (get rows id) 1)),\\n   :slot (fn [id f]\\n           (let [row (get rows id)]\\n             (nth row (get slot-position [(nth row 1) f]))))\\n   :check (fn [_id])})\\n\\n\\n(defn project-rows\\n  \\\"Project one canonical row set (`vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`'s `{:root\\n   id, :rows {id row}}`) to the register-shaped vector. The rows are\\n   validated (§7.4) first and a defect throws naming it. `origin` is nil\\n   or one of §2.5's ruled occurrence origins.\\n\\n   Returns `{:vector v :address A :provenance [[pc origin root path] …]}`,\\n   one provenance row per pc, `path` being the emitting node's §2.5\\n   structural path from the root row (row-position steps; a `nodes` item\\n   is a `[position i]` pair); a body's `:return` carries its lambda's\\n   path.\\\"\\n  ([bc] (project-rows bc nil))\\n  ([{:keys [root rows], :as bc} origin]\\n   (when-not (or (nil? origin) (vm/occurrence-origin? origin))\\n     (throw (ex-info \\\"Cannot project rows with a malformed occurrence origin\\\"\\n                     {:rule :origin, :origin origin})))\\n   (when-let [{:keys [rule path id]} (vm/validate-rows bc)]\\n     (throw (ex-info \\\"Cannot project rows that fail validation\\\"\\n                     (cond-> {:rule rule}\\n                       path (assoc :path path)\\n                       id (assoc :id id)))))\\n   (let [{:keys [code sources]} (flatten-tree (row-reader rows) root)]\\n     {:vector code,\\n      :address (ucf/code-address code),\\n      :provenance (mapv (fn [pc [_ path]] [pc origin root path])\\n                        (range)\\n                        sources)})))\\n\\n\\n(defn project\\n  \\\"`project-rows` ∘ `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`: a map AST to the\\n   register-shaped vector by the row lane.\\\"\\n  ([ast] (project ast nil))\\n  ([ast origin] (project-rows (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast) origin)))\\n\\nrg: docs/design/yang.python.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1239:|    |                                    | the VM's operational halts (budget |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1930:  budget** instead, raising the fatal with PHP's text, a recorded departure\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1932:  integer budget per run; omission means unlimited. Each entry to a `:loop` or\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1937:  blocking host operations; no generic VM instructions are charged.\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2102:|       | `pcntl_signal`, the safepoint    | stream never parks; budget        |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2103:|       | budget                           | exhaustion raises the pinned      |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2326:|                     | deterministic safepoint budget and  |                  |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2483:5. (B8.5) The deterministic safepoint budget as the `max_execution_time`\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_22\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_23\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The versioning obligations are also clearer: evidence schemas, lowering profiles, execution contracts, and module-manifest schemas are separate. Astra is checking which changes the addendum should require, while preserving the oracle round and current test lanes.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_24\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_24\",\"type\":\"collab_tool_call\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"sender_thread_id\":\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\",\"receiver_thread_ids\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"agents_states\":{},\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_25\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"**gpt-6-astra’s verdict: amend before owner signature.** The direction is endorsed; the draft needs four clarifications.\\n\\n1. **Attach provenance to UAST application occurrences; do not yet prescribe a canonical node field.** Existing architecture defines a call site as `[origin root-address path]` and excludes provenance from hashed code content. Occurrence side tables and instruction-provenance mappings already exist. The draft’s representation choice conflicts with that boundary. See [occurrence identity](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:659).\\n\\n   Replace item 2 with:\\n\\n   > Every frontend preserves caller-site provenance for post-expansion UAST application occurrences through a shared, language-neutral occurrence contract. The design round starts from existing occurrence and instruction-provenance relations. This addendum does not authorize a new canonical AST slot; any such proposal requires explicit justification and amendment of the occurrence/content boundary.\\n\\n2. **Separate the versioning obligations.** “A §8.1 contract revision” is too broad. Evidence schemas, lowering profiles, execution contracts, and module-manifest schemas have distinct revision rules. Existing machinery detects incompatibility; it does not automatically migrate artifacts. See [§8.1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1871) and [lowering profiles](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:861).\\n\\n   Require the design round to inventory affected producers, validators, lowering and reconstruction paths, derivation verification, publication, persisted artifacts, and mismatch behavior. Revise only the contracts actually changed.\\n\\n3. **Make adoption coverage and host verification explicit.** Include production `yang.clojure`: oracle instrumentation does not establish coverage of ordinary compilation paths. Inventory other `yang.*` entry points. Jolt inherits shared implementation changes, but still requires an applicable load/parity gate.\\n\\n   Replace the rollout wording with:\\n\\n   > Adoption covers production Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, and Python, with additional entry points inventoried during design. Each frontend has an acceptance gate; applicable JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt host lanes verify the resulting revision. Current sweeps, the J2 rerun, and the oracle round continue under their existing scopes.\\n\\n   Also describe instantiation #1 as **oracle-specific instrumentation**, since its signed scope already permits walker changes.\\n\\n4. **Activate R1 at signature and establish a precise dependency.** Owner scheduling validly activates R1; an already shipped second consumer is unnecessary. However, “lands with (2)/(3)” leaves acceptance ordering unclear.\\n\\n   Replace that wording with:\\n\\n   > By signing this addendum, the owner activates R1’s deferred work. The design round specifies the durable correspondence record and its additive relationship to versioned oracle evidence. Implementation and independent reconstruction complete before, or atomically with, acceptance of the first additional consumer that relies on it. The oracle round remains unblocked.\\n\\nThe design round should explicitly measure application counts, provenance and trace sizes, peak trace occupancy, instruction overhead, compilation/loading/query time, and memory. Separate static annotation costs from runtime tracing costs, and declare bounds before tagged runs. Its correctness gates should cover duplicate occurrences, macro expansion, helper calls, indirect and tail calls, and applicable suspension/resumption paths.\\n\\n**With these amendments, Astra endorses the addendum for owner signature as architectural direction and scheduling authority.** R1–R7 remain intact. No files were changed; this was a read-only review.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1100731,\"cached_input_tokens\":1038208,\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":3609,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":530}}\nDISPATCH_RC=0\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-RECORD.md\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/publication.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/publication.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc\ncollab/yang-python/1793410000000-orchestrator-yang-python-attribution-note.md\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/case_arms.edn\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/throw_sites.edn\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
