{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '382,510p;635,690p;785,925p'\"","aggregated_output":"   382\t  (`ast_walker.cljc:20-23`). The expander needs the fact that a particular\n   383\t  definition is a macro; that fact travels beside the tree as an\n   384\t  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\n   385\t- **`:eid`** (§4.4).\n   386\t- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\n   387\t  the tree names the root. No root fact exists in a row — the root row's\n   388\t  id is the root fact (§4.1).\n   389\t- **`t`, `m`, provenance.** Every provenance fact the codec or linearizer\n   390\t  writes today (`:yin.code/source`, `:yin.code/derived-from`) becomes a\n   391\t  ledger record (§8) or a side-table entry.\n   392\t\n   393\t**Occurrences.** Content identity names *what*; three further things\n   394\tname *where*, and each is a distinct coordinate:\n   395\t\n   396\t- A **structural path** names a place inside one tree: a vector of slot\n   397\t  indices from the root, with an index into a `nodes` slot written as a\n   398\t  pair `[slot i]`. `[]` is the root; `[2 [3 0]]` is the first operand of\n   399\t  the root's second slot. Slot indices are the row's positions, which the\n   400\t  §2.3 dictionary fixes against the map's key order, so a path reads\n   401\t  identically on both sides of the round trip. A path is a pure function\n   402\t  of the tree, stable across media and hosts. `[root-address path]`\n   403\t  separates two identical subtrees at two places in one tree, and nothing\n   404\t  more.\n   405\t- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\n   406\t  j]`, the program medium's identity, the batch coordinate on it (the\n   407\t  `:yin/source-batch` of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1), and the **member index**\n   408\t  `j` of the tree within the batch (§8.5's ordered batch carries several\n   409\t  trees). Two identical trees parsed from two files, admitted in two\n   410\t  batches, or sitting at two indices of one batch have one root address\n   411\t  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\n   412\t  source, and neither does a batch without its member index.\n   413\t  Here `medium` is the program medium's `:dao.stream/identity` and `batch`\n   414\t  is the admitting composition's fresh plain-data token, carried as\n   415\t  `:yin/source-medium` and `:yin/batch-token` in the batch envelope. A retry\n   416\t  of an unaccepted staged append keeps that token; a distinct admission\n   417\t  mints another. Neither coordinate is inferred from cursor internals or\n   418\t  content.\n   419\t- A **producing event** names one generated tree: the `:expand` event\n   420\t  (§8.4) whose output it is. Two expansion attempts with identical output\n   421\t  have one output address and two producing events. An event has two\n   422\t  identities, used in two places: a **log-local entity id**, which only a\n   423\t  declared reference attribute on the same log may name, and the\n   424\t  **address of its expansion record** (§8.2), which is portable.\n   425\t\n   426\tAn **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\n   427\t`origin` is `[:source medium batch j]` for admitted syntax and\n   428\t`[:expansion record-address]` for generated syntax. Every side table and\n   429\tevery provenance link that must distinguish places, sources, or attempts\n   430\tis keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\n   431\tindex travels in the origin uniformly: declarations, source positions,\n   432\tinstruction provenance, and initial expansion events all carry the same\n   433\t`[:source medium batch j]`. Joining a position table to a batch table on\n   434\troot address is not a substitute: it yields every batch-position\n   435\tcombination, which is exactly the ambiguity the origin coordinate\n   436\tremoves. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\n   437\tkey, because a key travels and the number does not (§8.4).\n   438\t\n   439\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   440\t| Side table             | Row                                                                                                                    | Keeper               |\n   441\t+========================+========================================================================================================================+======================+\n   442\t| source positions       | `[[:source medium batch j] root-address path file line col]`                                                           | the frontend that    |\n   443\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | parsed the file      |\n   444\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   445\t| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\n   446\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   447\t| instruction provenance | `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`                                                                        | the lowering (§5.3)  |\n   448\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   449\t| macro declarations     | `[[:source medium batch j] path]`: the definition at `path` of the batch's `j`-th tree is a macro definition; inside   | the frontend,        |\n   450\t|                        | the batch envelope this is written `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` (§8.5)                                             | consumed by the      |\n   451\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | expander             |\n   452\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   453\t| harvest catalogue      | `[[:source medium batch] h j path]`: occurrence `[j path]` belongs to the `h`-th **original definition** in the        | the frontend or the  |\n   454\t|                        | batch's admission order, plain or macro; one original definition shared at several paths has several rows with one     | §9.1 adapter,        |\n   455\t|                        | `h`. The macro-only declaration ordinal `k` is derived from this table and the declarations, not stored (§8.5)         | consumed by the      |\n   456\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | expander             |\n   457\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   458\t\n   459\t### 2.6 A walker-local note on runtime mutation\n   460\t\n   461\tThe frame re-scheme this section once carried is withdrawn as a design\n   462\trequirement (owner ruling, 2026-09-15): the walker stays on map ASTs, and\n   463\truntime `assoc` into a map node is legal, because the held node is the\n   464\tmachine's ephemeral image of the code, not a canonical value (§2.1), and\n   465\tnothing runtime-`assoc`ed is ever projected back to rows. The reasoning\n   466\tworth keeping, as a note and not a rule: today's cold arms `assoc`\n   467\t`:operator-evaluated?`/`:fn` (`ast_walker.cljc:213-217`) and `:evaluated`\n   468\t(`:226-237`), and the hot arms the same (`:532-541`, `:576-580`);\n   469\t`:operator-evaluated?` is redundant with the frame type, and a walker that\n   470\twanted the node read-only could keep every mutable field a sibling key of\n   471\tthe frame map — an implementation choice, available at any time, required\n   472\tby nothing here. Frame continuations remain runtime state, not syntax, as\n   473\tdo closures (`ast_walker.cljc:364-370`) and reified continuations\n   474\t(`ast_walker.cljc:422-427`). When continuation frames are themselves serialized or projected to datoms, the runtime keys the walker assoc'd into nodes must be stripped at that boundary.\n   475\t\n   476\t---\n   477\t\n   478\t## §3 The two boundary calls\n   479\t\n   480\t### 3.1 `:vm/store-update` is excluded from the canonical grammar\n   481\t\n   482\tThe node carries a host function in its `:fn` field and applies it inside\n   483\tthe syntax (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`). It is excluded on two grounds, and\n   484\tthey are sufficient: canonical content admits no host value (§2.2,\n   485\t`data`), and the node is absent from every persistent and portable form\n   486\talready, since the codec has no arm and throws on it (`v2.cljc:413-478`),\n   487\tthe linearizer lists it as unsupported (`linearize.cljc:19-22`), the\n   488\tsemantic VM has no opcode for it, and no test constructs one (a search of\n   489\t`test/` for the node type on 2026-09-14 found none). Its one reachable\n   490\tpath is an in-process, hand-built map AST handed straight to the walker;\n   491\tno row can carry it (§2.2, `data`), so it never crosses the boundary in\n   492\teither direction.\n   493\t\n   494\tThe orchestrator's alternative, a `:fn` slot restricted to a named\n   495\tprimitive symbol resolved and profile-checked per UCF §7.5.2, is not\n   496\tadopted: it would re-admit a node that no persistent form carries, for a\n   497\tconvenience no corpus program uses.\n   498\t\n   499\tA program that needs the behaviour writes it as a definition (`yin/def`,\n   500\tsyntax under Rule R, never a primitive) over a `:vm/store-get` operand:\n   501\t`(yin/def k (f (vm/store-get k) args…))`. That rewrite is equivalent to\n   502\tthe arm **only under stated conditions**, and this document does not claim\n   503\tit as a general semantics-preserving migration:\n   504\t\n   505\t- `f` resolves through `resolve-var`'s precedence, store, then\n   506\t  primitives, then modules (`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`), so the\n   507\t  rewrite is equivalent only when `f` is not shadowed by an enclosing\n   508\t  `:lambda`'s params at the site (so it resolves as a free name, through\n   509\t  the fallthrough, rather than to a local binding); `yin/def` itself is\n   510\t  never resolved and cannot be shadowed (Rule R);\n   635\t\n   636\t### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\n   637\t\n   638\tToday a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned\n   639\tentity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda\n   640\t(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it\n   641\t(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each\n   642\tcall-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\n   643\t\n   644\tContent addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one\n   645\t`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\n   646\taddress by construction and one row in the stored set, and a query \"which\n   647\tsites reference this lambda's content\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\n   648\tBecause this design retains named variables in the canonical tuple (§2, §5.1),\n   649\tthis structural sharing requires exact identity, not alpha-equivalence:\n   650\ttwo functions that are identical up to variable renaming project to\n   651\t*different* rows, since the parameter and variable names are part of the\n   652\thashed content. Only subtrees identical in structure *and* in every retained\n   653\tname collapse to one row. The row set holds each such distinct subtree once,\n   654\tand parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\n   655\t**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\n   656\t`:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the codec.\n   657\t\n   658\tWhat `:eid` also did, and content addresses do not, is name a *place*.\n   659\t\"This call site\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\n   660\tnot the site's content address; a query that needs distinct call sites\n   661\tjoins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\n   662\tlink that must survive a second identical site, a second identical file,\n   663\tor a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\n   664\taddresses serve sharing and cross-media correspondence of content;\n   665\toccurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\n   666\t\n   667\t### 4.5 Free variables are queried, not tagged\n   668\t\n   669\tAn earlier revision of this design split variable references into two\n   670\tnode types: `:variable` for names bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s\n   671\tparams, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n   672\tfree names, resolved through `resolve-var`'s store → primitives →\n   673\tmodules fallthrough. The motivation was static inspectability: knowing a\n   674\tprogram's free-name dependencies (for UCF §7.6.1's `:yin.k/requires`\n   675\tfixed point, and for capability checking before running untrusted\n   676\tcontent) without evaluating it.\n   677\t\n   678\tThat motivation does not require a second tag. Whether a `:variable` row\n   679\tis free or bound is exactly what a **query** over `$ast` (§6.1) can\n   680\tcompute: walk the row's ancestor chain (a `node`/`nodes` slot pointing at\n   681\ta row's own id is a parent-child edge, so the ancestor relation is a\n   682\trecursive Datalog rule over the row relation itself, needing no separate\n   683\toccurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\n   684\tin any enclosing `:lambda`'s `params`. If it does not, the name is free —\n   685\ta `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n   686\talready supports recursive rules (`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`'s\n   687\t`eval-rule`), so this is not a capability gap; it is a query the design\n   688\tnever needed to avoid.\n   689\t\n   690\tSplitting the tag instead of querying for the same fact bought nothing\n   785\tguards the `subvec` range, and the planner reorders only runs of\n   786\tadjacent pattern clauses. A positive call with `?root` unbound\n   787\tenumerates the roots where the name is bound at that path; inside `not`\n   788\tevery variable must be bound. Because the test is over path prefixes,\n   789\tnot row edges, nothing depends on which tag sits at a given path:\n   790\t`occurrence-walk-covers-the-whole-grammar` resolves binding through `:if`\n   791\tand `:stream/*` tags the row-only rules cannot walk. The indexer that\n   792\temits the occurrence tuples is `yin.vm/occurrences`.\n   793\t\n   794\tThrough the REPL, `(require 'dao.space.query)` makes `q` available over\n   795\t`$ast` and `$occ`; the shell ships no binding for the rule set, so the\n   796\tuser defines or pastes the literal above. The answer there is the raw\n   797\tfree set: it includes `yin/def` where a definition is present, which\n   798\t`yin.vm/free-names` removes in host code.\n   799\t\n   800\t**The definition operator is never free (Rule R).** `yin/def` is syntax,\n   801\tnever a name. A `:variable` row naming it is legal only as the operator\n   802\tslot of a two-operand application whose first operand is a literal\n   803\tsymbol other than `yin/def`; every other occurrence, a `:lambda` binding\n   804\tit, and a store key naming it are refused by `validate-rows` (S7.4).\n   805\t`yin.vm/free-names` never returns it, and no query over these rows\n   806\tcounts it as an obligation: a definition is recognized by syntax and\n   807\twrites its literal key, it does not resolve its operator.\n   808\t\n   809\t---\n   810\t\n   811\t## §5 The segment level\n   812\t\n   813\t### 5.1 The instruction vector is UCF §7.3.2\n   814\t\n   815\tThe segment's canonical form is the positional instruction tuple vector of\n   816\tUCF §7.3.2: one tuple per instruction, pc is the index, the\n   817\t[the deleted v3 design](./yin.vm.semantic.md#history) §2.4 table fixes each mnemonic's arity and operand\n   818\tkinds, defaults are saturated, refs are resolved pcs, and the header folds\n   819\taway. Its address is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`, the value\n   820\t[the deleted v3 design](./yin.vm.semantic.md#history) §2.2 already types `:yin.code/hash` as. The two\n   821\tlevels are uniform under this design: an AST row (§2.1) and an instruction\n   822\ttuple are both flat positional tuples, canonical and content-addressed,\n   823\tand the instruction vector was already this shape before the flat-row\n   824\truling clarified the AST level. To perfectly preserve program semantics\n   825\tacross boundaries, both the Universal Map AST and the Semantic Tuples\n   826\tstrictly retain named variables. Therefore, every contract name in this\n   827\tdocument means the Named Variable grammar: `\"v2\"` where it appears in\n   828\thistorical narrative, and `\"v3\"`, the current revision (Rule R adds the\n   829\t`:define` instruction and reserves `yin/def`), in normative text. This\n   830\tdesign otherwise adds\n   831\tnothing to that form and restates none of it.\n   832\t\n   833\t### 5.2 The hash chain is realized by derivation records\n   834\t\n   835\t```\n   836\ttree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\n   837\t```\n   838\t\n   839\tNeither value carries the other's address: the tree is the input, the\n   840\tvector excludes `:yin.code/derived-from` (UCF §7.3.2, *The header folds\n   841\taway*). The link is a **derivation record**, itself content-addressed\n   842\t(§8.2):\n   843\t\n   844\t```clojure\n   845\t{:yin.ledger/op       :derive\n   846\t :yin.ledger/input    <tree-address>\n   847\t :yin.ledger/output   <segment-address>\n   848\t :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\n   849\t :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-to-bytecode\"          ; the lowering profile, §5.2.1\n   850\t                       :yin.code/contract \"v3\"  ; UCF S7.3.3 contract\n   851\t                       :yin.k/version     0}}\n   852\t```\n   853\t\n   854\tIts address is published in the ledger (§8.3). The chain\n   855\t`record → input, record → output` is what \"the hash chain is the ledger\"\n   856\tmeans under this design: the chain lives in ledger records and refs, not\n   857\tin the code content, which rightly excludes it.\n   858\t\n   859\t#### 5.2.1 The lowering profile\n   860\t\n   861\tUCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\n   862\tthe transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.\n   863\tIts declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\n   864\ttwo deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\n   865\tdifferent, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable\n   866\tonly against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:\n   867\t\n   868\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   869\t| Pinned by the profile                 | Reference value for `\"ast-to-bytecode\"`                                                                                                 |\n   870\t+=======================================+================================================================================================================================+\n   871\t| input grammar                         | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document                                                                             |\n   872\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   873\t| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\n   874\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   875\t| emission rules                        | the §5.3 flattening table of [the deleted v3 design](./yin.vm.semantic.md#history), in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right)    |\n   876\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   877\t| label and body order                  | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were  |\n   878\t|                                       | encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`)                                                  |\n   879\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   880\t| terminators                           | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`)                                          |\n   881\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   882\t| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\n   883\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   884\t\n   885\tThe profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution\n   886\tcontract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\n   887\tprofile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the\n   888\texecution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\n   889\tchoice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured\n   890\tmap above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection\n   891\t(§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.\n   892\t\n   893\t#### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps\n   894\t\n   895\tA consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two\n   896\tdifferent things and reports them separately:\n   897\t\n   898\t1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\n   899\t   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the value. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and\n   900\t   says nothing about the derivation.\n   901\t2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's\n   902\t   `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it lowers the reconstructed map under that profile and verifies the resulting instruction-vector address against `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality\n   903\t   verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-\n   904\t   mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A\n   905\t   consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports\n   906\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` naming both profiles, the same outcome UCF\n   907\t   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\n   908\t   output vector if the vector's own execution contract is one it\n   909\t   implements, because content integrity (step 1) is independent of who\n   910\t   lowered it.\n   911\t\n   912\tTwo trees that lower to one vector under one profile yield two records\n   913\twith one output; one tree lowered under two profiles yields two records\n   914\twith two outputs, and neither is corruption.\n   915\t\n   916\t### 5.3 Instruction provenance\n   917\t\n   918\t`:yin.code/source` (`v2.cljc:326`, `linearize.cljc:225`) leaves the\n   919\tinstruction: UCF §7.3.2 excludes it from the vector, and the tuple form has\n   920\tnowhere else to put it. It becomes the side table `[segment-address pc\n   921\torigin root-address path]` of §2.5, emitted by the lowering beside the\n   922\tvector and keyed by the full occurrence key, so that two identical call\n   923\tsites, two identical files, two identical trees at two batch indices, or\n   924\ttwo expansion attempts map to distinct instruction ranges. The `origin`,\n   925\tincluding the member index `j` for admitted syntax, is an input to the\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1790,1930p'; rg -n 'schema|derivation|profile|provenance' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker*.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1790,1930p'; rg -n 'schema|derivation|profile|provenance' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker*.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"  1790\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1791\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1792\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1793\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1794\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1795\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1796\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n  1797\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n  1798\t  again.\n  1799\t\n  1800\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n  1801\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n  1802\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n  1803\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n  1804\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n  1805\tgrain.\n  1806\t\n  1807\t### 7.4 Transitive requires and cycles\n  1808\t\n  1809\tA module body may itself `(require 'bar)`. Under section 7.3 that is the\n  1810\tchild parking on its own `:link-request`, and it needs no further rule to\n  1811\tcomplete. One rule prevents deadlock: before starting a child for\n  1812\t`'bar`, the scheduler walks the install ancestry of the requiring task\n  1813\t(the chain of `:parent` link ids through `:installs`); if `'bar` is\n  1814\tinstalling anywhere on that chain, the require is `:require-cycle`\n  1815\tnaming the chain, and the child that asked is refused. A cycle is legal\n  1816\tonly when delivered as one unit by the linker's dependency closure.\n  1817\t\n  1818\tThe linker may resolve `:yin.module/requires` ahead of time from the\n  1819\tmanifest and deliver every image of the closure in one response, ordered\n  1820\tso that dependencies precede dependents and a strongly connected\n  1821\tcomponent arrives as one unit. This is Phase B7's dependency closure\n  1822\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` D5, D6); this document fixes only that the\n  1823\tresponse is a vector of `[manifest derivation image obligations]`\n  1824\tentries in install order, that the scheduler starts one child per entry\n  1825\tin that order, and that a cycle is one unit hashed with canonical\n  1826\tmember ordering. How SCC identity is minted stays open (section 12).\n  1827\t\n  1828\t## 8. The manifest registry\n  1829\t\n  1830\t### 8.1 The module manifest\n  1831\t\n  1832\tA manifest is content: one map, materialized in `dao.jing`, named by its\n  1833\taddress. It replaces the `{sym fn}` dictionary entry.\n  1834\t\n  1835\t```clojure\n  1836\t{:yin.module/name        'my.lib\n  1837\t :yin.module/schema      1                   ; this container's shape\n  1838\t :yin.module/contracts   {:yin.ast/code          \"v3\"   ; per format\n  1839\t                          :yin.semantic/code     \"v3\"\n  1840\t                          :yin.debruijn.code     \"b2\"\n  1841\t                          :yin.debruijn.register \"r2\"}\n  1842\t :yin.module/tree        :segment/...        ; the one canonical tree\n  1843\t :yin.module/derivations {:yin.semantic/code     :segment/...  ; record\n  1844\t                          :yin.debruijn.code     :segment/...  ; record\n  1845\t                          :yin.debruijn.register :segment/...} ; record\n  1846\t :yin.module/index       {H :segment/... R :segment/...}       ; optional\n  1847\t :yin.module/exports     #{'foo 'bar}\n  1848\t :yin.module/requires    {'other.lib :segment/...}             ; manifest\n  1849\t :yin.module/primitives  {'println :yin.k.pp/blake3-...}       ; profile\n  1850\t :yin.module/footprint   {:store-keys #{} :effects #{}}}       ; UCF 7.6.1\n  1851\t```\n  1852\t\n  1853\tRules:\n  1854\t\n  1855\t- **One tree.** `:yin.module/tree` is the root row id of the canonical\n  1856\t  AST the module was authored as. The `:yin.ast/code` image *is* this\n  1857\t  tree, so it has no derivation record.\n  1858\t- **One derivation record per lowered format.** Each value under\n  1859\t  `:yin.module/derivations` is the address of a content-addressed\n  1860\t  derivation record in the shape `yin.vm.ledger/derive-record` mints:\n  1861\t  `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree\n  1862\t  :yin.ledger/output identity :yin.ledger/function f\n  1863\t  :yin.ledger/profile {...}}`. The linker fetches the record (through a\n  1864\t  `:yin.ledger/record` format record: `segment-matches?` identity, a\n  1865\t  validator over these keys, no parts, no obligations) and checks\n  1866\t  `:yin.ledger/input` equals `:yin.module/tree` and `:yin.ledger/output`\n  1867\t  is the identity it then fetches. A record leading from a different\n  1868\t  tree, or to a different identity, is `:derivation-mismatch`. Four\n  1869\t  individually valid images from four different trees therefore cannot\n  1870\t  share a manifest, and a swapped image fails before load.\n  1871\t- **Three contracts, kept apart.** A manifest carries a schema version\n  1872\t  and a per-format contract map, and neither is the other:\n  1873\t  - `:yin.module/schema` versions the manifest container itself: which\n  1874\t    keys exist and what they mean. It is checked by the\n  1875\t    `:yin.module/manifest` format record's validator, which implements\n  1876\t    exactly one schema version; a manifest of another version is\n  1877\t    `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:schema`. It says nothing about any\n  1878\t    bytecode.\n  1879\t  - `:yin.module/contracts` maps each format the manifest lowered to\n  1880\t    the execution contract that format's image targets: the UCF stamp\n  1881\t    `\"v3\"` for the semantic vector and the tree, the stack lowering\n  1882\t    contract `\"b2\"` for the stack image, the register contract `\"r2\"`\n  1883\t    for the register image. These are the Rule R revisions; a manifest\n  1884\t    naming \"v2\", \"b1\", or \"r1\" is `:contract-mismatch`, with no\n  1885\t    migration. A format present under `:yin.module/derivations`\n  1886\t    must be present here; one that is not is `:descriptor-defect` with\n  1887\t    rule `:contract-missing`.\n  1888\t  - A format record's `:contract` (section 4.1) is the one execution\n  1889\t    contract that record implements, and a request's\n  1890\t    `:yin.link/contract` is the one the requester runs.\n  1891\t  **Contract before content.** Immediately after the manifest passes\n  1892\t  steps 2 to 4, `(get :yin.module/contracts format)` for the requested\n  1893\t  format is checked against the format record's `:contract`\n  1894\t  (`:contract-mismatch`), before the name check and before any\n  1895\t  derivation or image is fetched (section 4.2, step 0). A four-way\n  1896\t  manifest linking through the stack kernel therefore compares `\"b2\"`\n  1897\t  with `\"b2\"`, and the same manifest linking through the semantic\n  1898\t  kernel compares `\"v3\"` with `\"v3\"`; the tree's `\"v3\"` and the register\n  1899\t  image's `\"r2\"` play no part in either check. Revision r3 carried one\n  1900\t  `:yin.module/contract \"v2\"` and would have refused every stack and\n  1901\t  register link of a valid manifest; that was a defect.\n  1902\t- **Verifying and trusting are two policies, and verifying never\n  1903\t  trusts.** The linker's `:derivation` policy is one of `:verifying` or\n  1904\t  `:trusted`, chosen by the composition; there is no policy under which\n  1905\t  a verifying link accepts a claim it did not recompute.\n  1906\t  - Under `:verifying`, for every image it delivers the linker fetches\n  1907\t    the manifest's tree through the `:yin.ast/code` record (steps 2 to\n  1908\t    4, so the tree itself is verified, and no further: the tree's own\n  1909\t    obligations are the walker's business, scanned only when the tree is\n  1910\t    the requested image), re-lowers it under the exact\n  1911\t    per-format profile the derivation record names (`\"ast-to-bytecode\"`\n  1912\t    for `:yin.semantic/code`, the stack lowering for\n  1913\t    `:yin.debruijn.code`, the register lowering for\n  1914\t    `:yin.debruijn.register`, each pinned by its own\n  1915\t    `:yin.ledger/profile` map), and compares the recomputed identity to\n  1916\t    `:yin.ledger/output` (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 5.2.2, step\n  1917\t    2). Inequality is `:derivation-mismatch`. If the linker does not\n  1918\t    implement that exact profile, or the tree is `:absent`, or the tree\n  1919\t    fails its own verification, the link is refused\n  1920\t    `:unverified-derivation` naming the profile and what was missing.\n  1921\t    It is never downgraded to trust.\n  1922\t  - Under `:trusted`, the linker checks only the record's input and\n  1923\t    output addresses (the rule above) and reports `:trust :composition`\n  1924\t    in the outcome, exactly as B6's trusted fallback did and never\n  1925\t    silently. A composition that chooses `:trusted` has chosen to\n  1926\t    accept the publisher's lowering, and the outcome says so.\n  1927\t    `:trusted` skips **re-lowering**, never retrievability: the\n  1928\t    composition that serves under `:trusted` holds the manifest's\n  1929\t    `:yin.module/tree` address in its `source-index` and is responsible\n  1930\t    for the tree being retrievable from it (the publisher's store, or a\n25:   derivation record formats of section 8.1 are `manifest-format` and\n64:    :undeclared-free :module-name-mismatch :derivation-mismatch\n65:    :unverified-derivation})\n728:;; The manifest and derivation record formats (section 8.1)\n731:(def manifest-schema\n732:  \"The one module-manifest schema version this linker implements (section\n735:   version is a `:schema` defect, never migrated.\"\n740:  \"The closed key set of a schema-1 manifest: every key of section 8.1's\n742:  #{:yin.module/name :yin.module/schema :yin.module/contracts\n743:    :yin.module/tree :yin.module/derivations :yin.module/index\n748:(def ^:private derivation-formats\n749:  \"The formats a manifest carries derivation records for. The tree itself\n755:  \"The schema-1 manifest validator (section 8.1): one schema version, the\n759:   symbol-keyed, and every format named under `:yin.module/derivations`\n778:      (or (when (not= manifest-schema (:yin.module/schema manifest))\n779:            {:rule :schema})\n802:          (when-not (and (map? (:yin.module/derivations manifest))\n804:                                   (and (contains? derivation-formats (key e))\n806:                                 (:yin.module/derivations manifest)))\n807:            (shape :yin.module/derivations))\n811:                (keys (:yin.module/derivations manifest)))\n842:  \"The closed key set of a derivation record: exactly the keys\n845:    :yin.ledger/function :yin.ledger/profile})\n849:  \"The derivation-record validator (section 8.1): a record is exactly\n854:   profile map, no more and no less. Returns the defect map or nil.\"\n870:      (not (map? (:yin.ledger/profile record)))\n871:      (shape :yin.ledger/profile)\n878:   by it, its contract this record's one schema version. No parts, no\n881:   :contract            manifest-schema,\n893:  \"The `:yin.ledger/record` format record (section 8.1): a derivation\n909:(def stack-lowering-profile\n910:  \"The lowering profile this linker implements for\n913:   tree reconstructed first. A derivation record naming this profile\n916:  {:yin.lower/profile \"stack-lowering\",\n921:(def register-lowering-profile\n922:  \"The lowering profile this linker implements for\n926:  {:yin.lower/profile \"register-lowering\",\n931:(def ^:private implemented-profiles\n932:  \"The one profile per lowered format this linker recomputes under the\n934:   `yin.vm.ledger`'s published profile.\"\n935:  {:yin.semantic/code ledger/lowering-profile,\n936:   :yin.debruijn.code stack-lowering-profile,\n937:   :yin.debruijn.register register-lowering-profile})\n1001:   obligation is discharged only by a primitive of equal profile address\n1003:   (`module/host-export-profile`) -- a module obligation only by a linked\n1006:   profile; same name, other manifest -- resolves nothing, and neither\n1013:          profile (:profile obligation)]\n1014:      (when-not (and (some? profile)\n1016:                              (= profile (:yin.k/profile entry)))\n1017:                         (= profile (module/host-export-profile\n1043:   against the free env and store, then only by equal profile address or\n1376:   the `:verifying` derivation policy's fetch of a manifest's tree asks\n1446:   does. The name environment, authority, derivation, and fallback\n1599:   step 4, unscanned (`verify`'s five-arity, section 8.1's derivation\n2069:   carrying the profile address; a name under `:yin.module/requires` --\n2083:      {:name sym, :kind :primitive, :profile (get primitives sym)}\n2123:   canonical v4 vector. Nil when projection or derivation fails.\"\n2164:   (section 8.1): the profile the record names must be the one this\n2171:   a native derivation may name it as input. Returns `[state\n2172:   outcome]`, the outcome `{:derivation r :output o :tree t :trust\n2174:  [state drive format-kw derivation tree-addr]\n2175:  (let [profile (:yin.ledger/profile derivation)\n2176:        implemented (get implemented-profiles format-kw)]\n2177:    (if (not= profile implemented)\n2178:      [state (refused :unverified-derivation\n2179:                      {:format format-kw, :profile profile,\n2180:                       :implemented implemented, :missing :profile})]\n2184:          [state (refused :unverified-de