ever in C++.\n    13\t   No native pointer leaves `export-records`.\n    14\t\n    15\t   The calls are synchronous and `:blocking`, as §7.5 prescribes.\n    16\t\n    17\t   A worker (§7.4) holds an opaque native handle that owns the lexer and\n    18\t   parser DFA vectors and prediction-context caches its parses use; the\n    19\t   generated ATNs are shared, immutable. Its owner disposes it with\n    20\t   `close-worker` (a jolt-only addition: the other hosts' workers are\n    21\t   collected). A worker serves one thread at a time; a concurrent call on the\n    22\t   same worker is refused by the wrapper (status -5) and throws here.\n    23\t\n    24\t   The library is loaded on first use, not with this namespace (J5): a\n    25\t   missing, unloadable or wrong-layout library is recorded, `available?`\n    26\t   answers false, and asking for a worker throws the qualified\n    27\t   `unavailable-parser` diagnostic, so a host composing its frontend catalog\n    28\t   can leave Python out (never another parser) instead of failing to load.\n    29\t\n    30\t   Disclosed limits: the native library is the one Python3 grammar the\n    31\t   wrapper was built with, whatever the profile names; a worker nobody closes\n    32\t   keeps its native caches until the process exits.\"\n    33\t  (:require\n    34\t    [clojure.string :as str]\n    35\t    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n    36\t    [yang.antlr.export :as export]\n    37\t    [yang.antlr.packet :as packet]))\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t;; =============================================================================\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
