xcodebuild[66827:111365033] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-NukFx2ct' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-06 00:56:10.662 xcodebuild[66844:111365065]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-06 00:56:10.871 xcodebuild[66844:111365062] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n5854869c5117e3659d8ce3bc3f140d017d7b8687\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-68KqUmkV' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-06 00:56:12.241 xcodebuild[66854:111365134]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-06 00:56:12.451 xcodebuild[66854:111365133] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-PDpITuJ6' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-06 00:56:13.032 xcodebuild[66857:111365147]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-06 00:56:13.252 xcodebuild[66857:111365146] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n3eebe844e406ba56bc38a5f4b4a0098d811b5efd\n   917\t\n   918\t`:yin.code/source` (`v2.cljc:326`, `linearize.cljc:225`) leaves the\n   919\tinstruction: UCF §7.3.2 excludes it from the vector, and the tuple form has\n   920\tnowhere else to put it. It becomes the side table `[segment-address pc\n   921\torigin root-address path]` of §2.5, emitted by the lowering beside the\n   922\tvector and keyed by the full occurrence key, so that two identical call\n   923\tsites, two identical files, two identical trees at two batch indices, or\n   924\ttwo expansion attempts map to distinct instruction ranges. The `origin`,\n   925\tincluding the member index `j` for admitted syntax, is an input to the\n   926\tlowering, supplied by the composition that admitted or generated the\n   927\ttree; the lowering never infers it. Rendering an instruction range back to\n   928\tsource is a join over that table and the source-position table on the\n   929\toccurrence key, which is the query [the deleted v3 design](./yin.vm.semantic.md#history) §5.3 says it is;\n   930\tfor generated code the join goes through the producing event's record\n   931\taddress to its call occurrence (§8.4).\n   932\t\n   933\t---\n   934\t\n   935\t## §6 Querying\n   936\t\n   937\t### 6.1 The row relation and the two directions around it\n   938\t\n   939\tThe flat rows are the canonical stored form (§2.1), and `q` runs over\n   940\tthem directly — ruling 3 of 2026-09-14 was verified live over exactly\n   941\tthis shape. A stored set holds one row per distinct subtree: identical\n   942\tsubtrees project to the identical row, so the set is the tree's DAG\n   943\t(§4.4). Because a tag has one arity in the tree, it has one arity in the\n   944\trelation (tag arity plus one for the id). Rows of one tag are\n   945\thomogeneous, so a where-pattern for a tag has exactly that tag's arity\n   946\tand no padding. Rows from many trees union into one relation without\n   947\tcollision, because ids are addresses. An occurrence count is a count\n   948\tover parent slots or over the occurrence relation below, never over\n   949\trows.\n   950\t\n   951\t**The Dedicated AST Indexer:** This row relation (called `$ast` in queries) is maintained by the dedicated AST indexer (§1). The indexer is a `dao.stream` observer peer to the evaluators (§7.1); its input is the row batches of §7.1; its output is this row relation plus the occurrence relation below; it is distinct from `dao.space.index`, which indexes datoms only (§6.5); it is a projection keeper per §1's Query layer, so discarding it loses nothing.\n   952\t\n   953\t`map → rows` is the **codec boundary projection** (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`): strip the §2.5\n   954\texclusions, saturate per §2.4, positionalize per the §2.3 dictionary,\n   955\tmerkle per §4.1. It is the standing contract of the Encoder Observer, not a\n   956\tmigration device (§9.1).\n   957\t\n   958\t`rows → map` is **load-time reconstruction** (§7.1): validate the rows\n   959\t(§7.4), then rebuild the map AST through the ids. The precedent is\n   960\t`datoms->ast` (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:494-559`), which already\n2:  \"`project`: the Universal AST → the register-shaped canonical vector\n29:   projection naming the node.\"\n147:           (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot project node of type \" (tag id))\n188:(defn project-rows\n199:  ([bc] (project-rows bc nil))\n202:     (throw (ex-info \"Cannot project rows with a malformed occurrence origin\"\n205:     (throw (ex-info \"Cannot project rows that fail validation\"\n217:(defn project\n218:  \"`project-rows` ∘ `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`: a map AST to the\n220:  ([ast] (project ast nil))\n221:  ([ast origin] (project-rows (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast) origin)))\n239:    (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot project a host value in \" f)\n257:           (throw (ex-info (str \"Cannot project unsupported node \" t)\n283:(defn project-datoms\n295:      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot project a program with a dangling root\" error)))\n297:      (throw (ex-info \"Cannot project a program with no root\" {})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
